a_total": 6.74, "baseline_margin": -3.66, "analyst_adjustment": -0.34, "key_factors": ["R+5 district fundamentals favor incumbent Mike Carey", "Nearly all expert raters call it Likely R", "Democratic-sponsored Hart poll shows a one-point race, but sponsor lean and thin polling warrant caution", "Carey's large fundraising edge ($2.1M cash vs $250K)"], "rationale": "The district's R+5 lean, Carey's incumbency and financial advantage, and a Likely R consensus among raters point to a Republican edge, tempered only by a single Democratic-sponsored poll showing a dead heat and outside spending against Carey. Markets price Democrats near 25%, consistent with a modest Republican margin.", "watch": "An independent poll confirming the Hart result, or a broader national Democratic wave, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Carey (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's 15th District, anchored in Columbus's western suburbs and surrounding rural areas, leans Republican by about five points. Incumbent Mike Carey is seeking a third term against Democrat Don Leonard, a first-time candidate who has outraised expectations but remains badly outspent by the incumbent. Most nonpartisan raters place the race in the Likely Republican column, though two rate it Lean R and one calls it a Tossup.\n\nThe race has drawn national attention: Democratic outside groups have spent against Carey over votes on SNAP and Medicaid cuts, and a September Hart Research poll showed the candidates essentially tied. Still, the district's fundamentals, Carey's cash advantage, and prediction markets pricing his win odds near three in four suggest he remains favored, with the margin likely in the mid-single digits.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1732, "margin": -5.63}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.185, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1541, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-